</div></section></section><section aria-label="Article content"><section class="body main-article-body"><section class="abstract" id="Abs1"><h2>Abstract</h2>
<section id="sec1"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Introduction</h3>
<p id="Par1">Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease. 611, a humanized monoclonal antibody, selectively targets the interleukin (IL)-4 receptor alpha, thereby inhibiting the signaling of both interleukin (IL)-4 and IL-13. This phase 2 study aimed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of 611 in Chinese adults with moderate-to-severe AD.</p></section><section id="sec2"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Methods</h3>
<p id="Par2">This randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 2 study was conducted between October 2022 and September 2023. Eligible patients with moderate-to-severe AD were randomly assigned in a 1:1:1 ratio to receive 611 at a dose of either 300 mg (loading dose of 600 mg) every 2 weeks (Group A) or 300 mg (loading dose of 600 mg) every 4 weeks (Group B), or placebo every 2 weeks for 16 weeks, followed by an 8-week follow-up period. The primary efficacy endpoint was the proportion of patients achieving at least a 75% reduction in the Eczema Area and Severity Index (EASI-75) score at week 16. The safety and pharmacodynamics were also assessed.</p></section><section id="sec3"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Results</h3>
<p id="Par3">After 16 weeks of treatment, 60.0% of patients in Group A and 48.8% in Group B achieved EASI-75, both significantly higher than the placebo group (15.6%, <em>p</em> &lt; 0.01). Additionally, 611 at both doses significantly improved the Investigator’s Global Assessment (IGA) scores, peak pruritus numerical rating scale (NRS), and other efficacy endpoints. Patients receiving 611 demonstrated significant reductions in serum thymus activation-regulated chemokine (TARC) and total serum immunoglobulin E (IgE) levels. The incidence of treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) was similar across all dosage groups. The most common 611-related TEAE is upper respiratory tract infections. No new safety concerns were identified.</p></section><section id="sec4"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Conclusion</h3>
<p id="Par4">611 demonstrated a high efficacy and a favorable safety profile in patients with moderate-to-severe AD.</p></section><section id="sec5"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Trial Registration</h3>
<p id="Par5">ClinicalTrials.gov, <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT05544591" class="usa-link" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCT05544591</a>.</p></section><section id="sec6"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Supplementary Information</h3>
<p>The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1007/s13555-025-01368-4.</p></section><section id="kwd-group1" lang="en" class="kwd-group"><p><strong>Keywords:</strong> Atopic dermatitis, Chinese patients, 611, Interleukin-4 receptor alpha, Eczema area, Severity index</p></section></section><section id="d33e470"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Key Summary Points</h2>
<section class="tw xbox font-sm" id="Taba"><div class="tbl-box p" tabindex="0"><table class="content" frame="hsides" rules="groups"><tbody>
<tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease with significant disease burden and limited treatment options for moderate-to-severe cases. An unmet need remains for novel biologic treatments that effectively target the underlying pathophysiology of AD while maintaining a favorable safety profile.</td></tr>
<tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">This study aimed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of 611, a humanized monoclonal antibody targeting interleukin-4 receptor alpha (IL-4Rα), in Chinese adults with moderate-to-severe AD.</td></tr>
<tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">Treatment with 611 demonstrated significant improvement in disease severity as measured by the Eczema Area and Severity Index (EASI-75), Investigator’s Global Assessment (IGA) 0/1 response, and reductions in pruritus and inflammatory biomarkers compared to placebo. The incidence of adverse events, including treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) and serious adverse events (SAEs), was comparable across treatment and placebo groups, indicating a favorable safety profile.</td></tr>
<tr><td align="left" colspan="1" rowspan="1">This study supports the potential of 611 as an effective therapeutic option for moderate-to-severe AD and highlights the need for further investigation in diverse populations to confirm its long-term efficacy and safety.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/Taba/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div></section></section><section id="Sec1"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Introduction</h2>
<p id="Par7">Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic inflammatory skin disease characterized by eczematous lesions and intense pruritus. In Chinese adults, the 1-year prevalence of adult AD was 6.13%, and the point prevalence of AD was 2.42% [<a href="#CR1" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR1">1</a>]. The point prevalence of AD based on clinical diagnosis by dermatologists was 12.94% overall, ranging from 9.00% to 24.69% between metropolises for preschool children in China [<a href="#CR2" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR2">2</a>–<a href="#CR4" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR4">4</a>]. The pathogenesis of AD involves a complex interplay between environmental and genetic factors, resulting in immune dysregulation [<a href="#CR5" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR5">5</a>]. Major immune abnormalities include allergen presentation, T-helper 2 cell (Th2)-driven responses, impaired regulatory T cells, excessive immunoglobulin E (IgE) production, and increased eosinophils [<a href="#CR6" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR6">6</a>–<a href="#CR10" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR10">10</a>]. Type 2 inflammation, primarily mediated by interleukin (IL)−4 and IL-13, is thought to play a central role in AD pathogenesis [<a href="#CR11" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR11">11</a>]. Elevated IL-4 and IL-13 levels are associated with AD severity, facilitating IgE production, macrophage activation, eosinophil recruitment, and pruritus through IL-4Rα signaling in neurons [<a href="#CR12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR12">12</a>].</p>
<p id="Par8">Dupilumab, a fully human monoclonal antibody, targets the IL-4/IL-13 signaling to inhibit a major source of inflammation associated with AD. Since its approval in 2020 by the National Medical Products Administration (NMPA), dupilumab has been used to treat moderate-to-severe AD in China. 611 is a recombinant humanized immunoglobulin-G4 (IgG4) monoclonal antibody that inhibits IL-4 and IL-13 signaling. Preclinical and phase 1 clinical studies have validated the safety and tolerability of subcutaneous administration of 611 in healthy subjects. This phase 2 study aimed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of 611 in patients with moderate-to-severe AD.</p></section><section id="Sec2"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Methods</h2>
<section id="Sec3"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Study Design, Patients, and Treatment</h3>
<p id="Par9">This multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 2 trial (ClinicalTrials.gov, <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT05544591" class="usa-link" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCT05544591</a>) was conducted across 23 sites in China. This study adhered to the ethical principles of the Declaration of Helsinki for medical research involving human subjects and was conducted in accordance with Good Clinical Practice (GCP) guidelines. Before the study commenced, the clinical trial protocol, case report forms, informed consent forms, and other related documents were approved by the Peking University People’s Hospital Ethics Committee (approval number: 2022PHC022-001). All patients provided written informed consent for participation in the study. The study duration for each participant was 28 weeks, including a screening period of up to 4 weeks, a 16-week treatment period, and an 8-week follow-up period. Patients aged 18–75 years with moderate-to-severe AD were enrolled. Inclusion criteria included a baseline Investigator’s Global Assessment (IGA) score of ≥ 3, Eczema Area and Severity Index (EASI) score of ≥ 16, ≥ 10% body surface area (BSA) of AD involvement, and a weekly average peak pruritus numerical rating scale (NRS) score of ≥ 4. Detailed inclusion and exclusion criteria are outlined in Supplementary Table S1. After screening, eligible subjects were stratified by IGA scores (3 or 4) and randomly assigned in a 1:1:1 ratio to the following groups: Group A (611 300 mg every 2 weeks with a 600 mg loading dose), Group B (611 300 mg every 4 weeks with a 600 mg loading dose), or placebo. All participants used emollients at least 7 days before randomization and continued this regimen throughout the study.</p></section><section id="Sec4"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Endpoints and Assessments</h3>
<section id="Sec5"><h4 class="pmc_sec_title">Efficacy Assessments</h4>
<p id="Par10">The primary efficacy endpoint was the proportion of patients achieving at least a 75% reduction in the Eczema Area and Severity Index (EASI-75) score at week 16. Secondary endpoints included the proportion of participants achieving EASI-50, EASI-90, IGA score of 0 or 1 (0/1) with ≥ 2 point reduction, and ≥ 4 points or ≥ 3 points in the weekly average peak pruritus NRS score from baseline to week 16. The Dermatology Life Quality Index (DLQI) and Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure (POEM) were also evaluated. (A list of all efficacy endpoints is provided in Supplementary Table S2).</p></section><section id="Sec6"><h4 class="pmc_sec_title">Safety Assessments</h4>
<p id="Par11">Safety assessment included treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs), vital signs, physical examinations, electrocardiograms (ECGs), and clinical laboratory test results.</p></section></section><section id="Sec7"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Biological Markers</h3>
<p id="Par12">Pharmacodynamic markers included TARC, total serum IgE, peripheral blood eosinophil count (EOS), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH).</p></section><section id="Sec8"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Statistical Analysis</h3>
<p id="Par13">The sample size calculation was based on the primary endpoint (the percentage of subjects achieving an EASI-75 response at week 16). Assuming EASI-75 response rates of 49.2% for 611 and 11.5% for the placebo group at week 16, a sample size of 90 subjects with a randomization ratio of 1:1:1 was required to achieve a stated power of 80% to show superiority of 611 over placebo at a significance level of <em>α</em> = 0.05 (two-sided) accounting for a 10% dropout rate. The calculation is based on Fisher’s exact test for the binomial distribution using PASS 2019 software.</p>
<p id="Par14">The primary analysis and binary endpoints were conducted in the Intent-to-Treat (ITT) analysis set, which included all randomized subjects, using the Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel (CMH) test adjusted for stratification factors [baseline IGA score (3 or 4)] to compare the proportions of subjects between treatment groups. Intercurrent events, including the use of rescue therapy, administration of prohibited medications affecting the interpretation, or discontinuation of treatment due to lack of efficacy, were classified as non-responders from that time. Missing data were imputed using the non-responder imputation (NRI).</p>
<p id="Par15">For continuous efficacy endpoints (e.g., a change from baseline in EASI score), an analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) model was employed to estimate the least square means (LSM) of changes from baseline to week 16 while controlling for baseline covariates and stratification factors. The intercurrent events, similar to the primary endpoint, were considered missing and imputed using the baseline observation carried forward (BOCF) from that time. Missing data were imputed using multiple imputation (MI), and the statistical inferences obtained from all imputed data were combined using Rubin’s rule. Safety analysis was conducted using descriptive statistics in the safety population, including all randomized subjects who received investigational drugs by treatment group.</p></section></section><section id="Sec9"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Results</h2>
<section id="Sec10"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Baseline Characteristics</h3>
<p id="Par16">A total of 120 subjects were screened, and 30, 31, and 32 subjects were assigned to Group A, Group B, and the placebo group, respectively (Fig. <a href="#Fig1" class="usa-link">1</a>). Nine patients withdrew from the study predominantly because of lack of efficacy (1 patient each in Groups A and B, and 7 patients in the placebo group).</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="Fig1"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 1.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><a class="tileshop" target="_blank" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&amp;p=PMC3&amp;id=11971068_13555_2025_1368_Fig1_HTML.jpg"><img class="graphic zoom-in" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/34b2/11971068/6ebe741c75a0/13555_2025_1368_Fig1_HTML.jpg" loading="lazy" id="MO1" height="301" width="746" alt="Fig. 1"></a></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/Fig1/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Patient disposition. Flowchart showing the enrollment, randomization, and follow-up of patients in the phase II trial. A total of 120 patients were screened, with 93 randomized into three groups: Group A (611 300 mg every 2 weeks), Group B (611 300 mg every 4 weeks), and placebo. Nine patients discontinued the study because of lack of efficacy, predominantly in the placebo group</p></figcaption></figure><p id="Par17">Demographics and baseline clinical characteristics, including gender, age, height, weight, and BMI, were well balanced across the treatment groups (Table <a href="#Tab1" class="usa-link">1</a>). The proportion of subjects with an IGA score of 3 was 56.7%, 58.1%, and 56.3% in Group A, Group B, and the placebo group, respectively. The mean baseline EASI scores were 28.7, 26.2, and 28.6 in Group A, Group B, and the placebo group, respectively. The mean baseline weekly average peak pruritus NRS scores were 7.3, 6.9, and 7.7 in Group A, Group B, and the placebo group, respectively.</p>
