<div class="tw-foot p"><div class="fn" id="_fn_p21">
<p>Data are presented as mean (SD) unless stated otherwise</p>
<p><em>BMI</em> body mass index, <em>IGA</em> Investigator’s Global Assessment, <em>EASI</em> Eczema Area and Severity Index, <em>BSA</em> body surface area, <em>AD</em> atopic dermatitis, <em>NRS</em> numerical rating scale, <em>DLQI</em> Dermatology Life Quality Index, <em>POEM</em> patient-oriented eczema measure</p>
</div></div></section></section><section id="Sec11"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Efficacy Endpoints</h3>
<p id="Par18">At week 16, 60.0% patients in Group A and 48.4% patients in Group B achieved EASI-75, which were significantly higher than in the placebo group (15.6%, <em>p</em> &lt; 0.01 for both comparisons with placebo) (Fig. <a href="#Fig2" class="usa-link">2</a>A). Moreover, 33.3% patients in Group A and 29.0% patients in Group B achieved EASI-90, which were significantly higher than in the placebo group (9.4%, <em>p</em> &lt; 0.05 for both comparisons with placebo). The LSM percentage changes in EASI scores from baseline to week 16 were − 69.2% (95% CI − 81.36%, − 57.06%) in Group A, − 68.5% (95% CI − 80.48%, − 56.47%) in Group B, and − 23.2% (95% CI − 34.86%, − 11.58%) in the placebo group (Fig. <a href="#Fig2" class="usa-link">2</a>B).</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="Fig2"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 2.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><a class="tileshop" target="_blank" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&amp;p=PMC3&amp;id=11971068_13555_2025_1368_Fig2_HTML.jpg"><img class="graphic zoom-in" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/34b2/11971068/679874bd09da/13555_2025_1368_Fig2_HTML.jpg" loading="lazy" id="MO2" height="199" width="746" alt="Fig. 2"></a></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/Fig2/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Efficacy outcomes of 611 at week 16. <strong>A</strong> Proportion of patients achieving Eczema Area and Severity Index (EASI-75) in Group A, Group B, and placebo groups. Both Group A (60.0%) and Group B (48.4%) showed significantly higher response rates compared to placebo (15.6%; <em>p</em> &lt; 0.01). <strong>B</strong> Least square mean (LSM) percentage change in EASI scores from baseline to week 16. Group A (− 69.2%; 95% CI − 81.36%, − 57.06%) and Group B (− 68.5%; 95% CI − 80.48%, − 56.47%) demonstrated significantly greater reductions compared to placebo (− 23.2%; 95% CI − 34.86%, − 11.58%). Data are presented as mean ± standard error</p></figcaption></figure><p id="Par19">By week 16, the proportions of subjects achieving an IGA score of 0/1 with ≥ 2 point reduction from baseline were 33.3% in Group A and 35.5% in Group B, which were significantly higher than in the placebo group (9.4%, <em>p</em> &lt; 0.05 for both comparisons with placebo) (Fig. <a href="#Fig3" class="usa-link">3</a>A). The proportions of subjects achieving a ≥ 4-point reduction from baseline in the weekly average peak pruritus scores were 46.7% in group A and 45.2% in group B, which were significantly higher than in the placebo group (15.6%, <em>p</em> &lt; 0.05 for both comparisons with placebo) (Fig. <a href="#Fig3" class="usa-link">3</a>B). The LSM changes in DLQI scores from baseline to week 16 were − 4.8 (95% CI − 6.69, − 2.85), − 5.5 (95% CI − 7.30, − 3.79), and − 3.1 (95% CI − 4.83, − 1.44) for Group A, Group B, and the placebo group, respectively.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="Fig3"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 3.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><a class="tileshop" target="_blank" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&amp;p=PMC3&amp;id=11971068_13555_2025_1368_Fig3_HTML.jpg"><img class="graphic zoom-in" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/34b2/11971068/2f616f9e2031/13555_2025_1368_Fig3_HTML.jpg" loading="lazy" id="MO3" height="202" width="746" alt="Fig. 3"></a></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/Fig3/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Improvements in Investigator’s Global Assessment (IGA) scores and peak pruritus numerical rating scale (NRS). <strong>A</strong> Proportion of patients achieving an IGA score of 0/1 with ≥ 2 point reduction from baseline. Group A (33.3%) and Group B (35.5%) exhibited significantly higher proportions compared to placebo (9.4%; <em>p</em> &lt; 0.05). <strong>B</strong> Proportion of patients achieving ≥ 4-point reduction in weekly average peak pruritus NRS scores. Group A (46.7%) and Group B (45.2%) showed significant improvements versus placebo (15.6%; <em>p</em> &lt; 0.05)</p></figcaption></figure></section><section id="Sec12"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Safety Endpoints</h3>
<p id="Par20">The incidence of TEAEs was similar between 611 and the placebo (63.3% in Group A and 64.5% in Group B vs. 65.6% in the placebo group), with most being TEAEs of Grades 1 or 2 severity (Table <a href="#Tab2" class="usa-link">2</a>). In Group A, two subjects experienced Grade 3 TEAEs: one subject had elevated creatine phosphokinase levels, which normalized after reducing physical activity without treatment and was regarded as unrelated to 611. The other subject was found to have left lung cancer during the follow-up period post-treatment. It was regarded as unrelated to 611. In Group B, one subject experienced two Grade 3 TEAEs: the first event was pneumonia occurring during the treatment period, which improved after treatment and was regarded as unrelated to 611; the second event was dizziness, occurring during the safety follow-up period, which improved after treatment and was regarded as unrelated to 611.</p>
