<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/Tab2/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p"><div class="fn" id="_fn_p29"><p><em>TEAE</em> treatment-emergent adverse event, <em>SAE</em> serious adverse event</p></div></div></section><p id="Par21">The incidence of TEAEs associated with the investigational drug was slightly higher in both 611 treatment groups (26.7% and 16.1%) compared to the placebo group (12.5%), and the severity was all Grade 1 or 2. Upper respiratory tract infections were the most common treatment-related TEAEs in both 611 treatment groups, with 6.7% in group A and 3.2% in group B.</p>
<p id="Par22">No significant abnormalities were detected in physical examinations, vital signs, ECGs, or laboratory tests across the three groups.</p></section><section id="Sec13"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Change in Biological Markers</h3>
<p id="Par23">At week 16, the median percent reductions from baseline in TARC levels were 60.8%, 58.8%, and 28.9% in Group A, Group B, and the placebo group, respectively. Treatment with 611 resulted in a rapid and sustained reduction in TARC levels. By week 24, approximately 10–12 weeks after the last dose, TARC levels gradually returned to baseline values.</p>
<p id="Par24">At week 16, the median percent reductions in total serum IgE levels were 49.7%, 36.6%, and 0% in Group A, Group B, and the placebo group, respectively. The total serum IgE levels showed a gradual decrease with treatment. No significant differences in EOS and LDH were detected between the 611 treatment and placebo groups.</p></section></section><section id="Sec14"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Discussion</h2>
<p id="Par25">This study demonstrated that 611 was effective in patients with moderate-to-severe AD. The EASI-75, EASI-90, and IGA 0/1 in the two treatment groups were superior over the placebo group. The regimen of 600 mg + 300 mg Q2W showed a more robust and rapid response in achieving IGA 0/1, whereas the regimen of 600 mg + 300 mg Q4W exhibited comparable improvement at week 16. The 300 mg Q2W was selected as the preferred regimen during the core treatment period, whereas the 300 mg Q4W was adopted during the maintenance period in the Phase 3 clinical trial (<a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/show/NCT06173284" class="usa-link" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCT06173284</a>).</p>
<p id="Par26">Subjects receiving treatment with 611 showed notable improvements in itch symptoms as early as week 2, with 10% and 16.1% of subjects in Groups A and B achieving an NRS score ≥ 4, which was similar to those of the dupilumab Q2W group (9% ~ 11% [<a href="#CR13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR13">13</a>]). By week 4, 23.3% and 12.9% of subjects in Groups A and B achieved an NRS score ≥ 4 (dupilumab Q2W: 16%–23% [<a href="#CR13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR13">13</a>]). By week 16, 46.7% and 45.2% of subjects in Groups A and B, respectively, achieved an NRS score ≥ 4 (dupilumab Q2W: 36% ~ 41% [<a href="#CR13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR13">13</a>]). Overall, receiving 611 every other week demonstrated similar efficacy to dupilumab in relieving patients’ symptoms. The incidence of rescue therapy was significantly lower in 611 treatment groups compared to the placebo group. The proportions of subjects who had used rescue therapy at least once were 16.7% (5/30), 12.9% (4/31), and 31.3% (10/32) in Group A, B, and placebo, respectively.</p>
<p id="Par27">Common adverse reactions associated with dupilumab in clinical studies for atopic dermatitis include injection site reactions (including erythema, edema, pruritus, pain, and inflammation), conjunctivitis, allergic conjunctivitis, arthralgia, oral herpes, and eosinophilia. In this study, only 1.6% of subjects treated with 611 experienced injection site reactions and injection site erythema related to the experimental drug (vs. dupilumab SOLO 1 and SOLO 2—injection site reactions: 8%–19%), and 1.6% of subjects treated with 611 experienced allergic conjunctivitis (vs. dupilumab SOLO 1 and SOLO 2—conjunctivitis: 3%–5%); all of the above were significantly lower than those found in SOLO 1 and SOLO 2. The lower incidence of conjunctivitis observed with 611 compared to dupilumab may be attributed to differences in molecular structure, binding affinity, or immune modulation beyond IL-4Rα inhibition. While both 611 and dupilumab target IL-4Rα, subtle variations in pharmacokinetics and immunogenicity may contribute to differences in adverse event profiles. Further investigations, including comparative clinical and mechanistic studies, are necessary to elucidate the underlying reasons for these observed differences.</p>
<p id="Par28">TARC and IgE are biological markers indicative of type 2 inflammation [<a href="#CR14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR14">14</a>]. 611 demonstrated a significant effect on the decrease of TARC and IgE levels. This finding further supports the specific mechanism of action of 611 in inhibiting IL-4 and IL-13 signaling pathways. Changes in EOS and LDH were minimal and showed a weak correlation with clinical improvements.</p>
<p id="Par29">AD exhibits genetic and phenotypic variations across different populations. Several studies have reported that East Asian patients with AD, including those in China, often present with unique clinical features, such as a higher prevalence of lichenification, the highest levels of TH17/TH22 activation among all ethnic groups, higher serum IgE levels compared to European populations, and a higher frequency of filaggrin gene mutations than African populations, which are closely associated with impaired skin barrier function [<a href="#CR15" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR15">15</a>]. These genetic and phenotypic differences may contribute to variations in AD pathogenesis and treatment responses. Furthermore, studies have shown that Asian patients with AD exhibit more pronounced type 2 and type 17 inflammatory responses, which may influence therapeutic efficacy [<a href="#CR16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR16">16</a>]. Therefore, although the efficacy and safety of 611 in Chinese patients align with global findings on IL-4Rα inhibitors, further investigations in diverse populations are necessary to establish its broader applicability.</p>
<p id="Par30">While this study provides valuable insights into the efficacy and safety of 611 in Chinese adults with moderate-to-severe AD, several limitations should be considered. First, this study was conducted exclusively in a Chinese adult population, which may limit the generalizability of the findings to other ethnic groups. Further research is needed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of 611 in a more diverse global population, including pediatric patients. Second, the relatively short follow-up period (16 weeks of treatment plus an 8-week follow-up period) restricts the ability to assess the long-term efficacy and safety of 611, particularly regarding potential adverse events that may emerge with prolonged use. Two ongoing Phase 3 studies with a 52-week treatment period are currently being conducted to further evaluate the long-term efficacy and safety of 611. Finally, while the study assessed key clinical endpoints such as EASI-75, IGA 0/1, and pruritus reduction, further investigation is required to determine the broader applicability of 611 in clinical practice.</p></section><section id="Sec15"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Conclusion</h2>
<p id="Par31">611 showed good efficacy and favorable safety profiles in Chinese adults with moderate-to-severe AD, achieving significant improvements across multiple efficacy endpoints compared to the placebo group. The results of this Phase 2 study underscore the potential of 611 as an IL-4Rα antibody for treating AD in adult patients, offering a promising new therapeutic option for those with moderate-to-severe AD.</p></section><section id="Sec16"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Supplementary Information</h2>
<p>Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material.</p>
